---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Experiment 7.3: Newton's Third Law">
    <main class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8">
        <div class="experiment-header">
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-center text-blue-700 mb-2">EXPERIMENT 7.3</h1>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center text-blue-700 mb-8">NEWTON'S THIRD LAW</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Email Collection Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
                <h4 class="font-bold mb-2">Student Information:</h4>
                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div>
                        <label for="studentName" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Name:</label>
                        <input type="text" id="studentName" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg" placeholder="Your Name">
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <label for="studentEmail" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Email:</label>
                        <input type="email" id="studentEmail" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg" placeholder="your.email@example.com">
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="mt-4">
                    <label for="teacherEmail" class="block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2">Teacher's Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="teacherEmail" class="w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg" value="chuck@c00lnerd.com" placeholder="teacher.email@example.com">
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Purpose Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">PURPOSE:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">To investigate action and reaction forces and observe Newton's third law of motion.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Materials Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">MATERIALS:</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-4">
                <li>A plastic, 2 liter bottle</li>
                <li>A stopper that fits the bottle (It could be rubber or cork, but you cannot use the screw-on cap. It has to be something that plugs up the opening of the bottle but can be pushed out by a pressure buildup inside the bottle. Modeling clay can work as well. You could also try a large wad of gum, as long as the gum has dried out and has the texture of firm rubber.)</li>
                <li>A cup of vinegar</li>
                <li>Two teaspoons of baking soda</li>
                <li>Aluminum foil</li>
                <li>Four pencils</li>
                <li>Eye protection such as safety goggles or safety glasses</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- Safety Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <div class="bg-yellow-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400 p-4">
                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-yellow-700">SAFETY:</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-0">
                    <li>Always wear eye protection when conducting this experiment.</li>
                    <li>This experiment should be performed outdoors as it is loud and messy.</li>
                    <li>Never point the bottle at yourself or others.</li>
                    <li>Stand to the side of the bottle, not in front of or behind it.</li>
                    <li>Keep a safe distance from the bottle once the stopper is in place.</li>
                    <li>Clean up all materials properly after the experiment.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Background Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">BACKGROUND:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">You will want to do this experiment outside, because it is loud and messy!</p>
            
            <p class="mb-4">Newton's Third Law of Motion states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. This means that when one object exerts a force on a second object, the second object exerts an equal force in the opposite direction on the first object.</p>
            
            <p class="mb-4">In this experiment, we will observe Newton's Third Law in action through a chemical reaction that produces gas, creating pressure inside a closed container. When the pressure builds up enough to push out the stopper, the escaping gas pushes in one direction (action), and the bottle moves in the opposite direction (reaction).</p>
            
            <p class="mb-4">The chemical reaction between vinegar (acetic acid) and baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) produces carbon dioxide gas. As this gas builds up in the sealed bottle, pressure increases until it forces the stopper out, demonstrating Newton's Third Law.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Question Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">QUESTION:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">What happens when a chemical reaction produces a lot of pressure inside a closed container? How can Newton's third law explain this?</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Hypothesis Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">HYPOTHESIS:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">Write your prediction for what will happen when a chemical reaction causes a pressure buildup in a closed container:</p>
            <textarea id="hypothesis" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="4" placeholder="Your hypothesis..."></textarea>
        </section>

        <!-- Procedure Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">PROCEDURE:</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 mb-4 space-y-4">
                <li>
                    <p>Pour about a cup of vinegar into the 2 L bottle.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Put the four pencils on the ground parallel to each other and about 2 inches apart. Lay the bottle on its side on top of the pencils. Do this carefully so that none of the vinegar spills out.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Take the aluminum foil and make a long, thin trough. The trough should be thin enough to fit inside the mouth of the bottle.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Once you have made the trough, fill it with 2 teaspoons of baking soda.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Gently push the trough into the bottle, so that it floats on top of the vinegar. Try to spill as little baking soda as possible.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="text-red-600 font-bold">Be careful to stay on the side of the bottle. You should not be in front of or behind it! Carefully use the stopper to plug up the mouth of the bottle.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Staying to one side of the bottle, roll the bottle to the side, allowing the baking soda to mix with the vinegar. For best results, make sure the bottle stays on the pencils.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p class="text-red-600 font-bold">Stand away to one side of the bottle. BE SURE TO STAY TO THE SIDE OF THE BOTTLE. DO NOT GET IN FRONT OF OR BEHIND IT!</p>
                    <div class="mt-4">
                        <label for="observations" class="block font-bold mb-2">Record what happens:</label>
                        <textarea id="observations" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="4" placeholder="Your observations..."></textarea>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Clean up and put everything away.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- Video Demonstration Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">VIDEO DEMONSTRATION:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">Watch this video demonstration of Newton's Third Law experiment:</p>
            
            <div class="bg-blue-50 p-6 rounded-lg">
                <div class="flex justify-center">
                    <div class="w-full max-w-4xl">
                        <div class="relative w-full" style="padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0;">
                            <iframe 
                                class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-full h-full rounded-lg shadow-lg"
                                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/NXk0EAC8jAQ"
                                title="Newton's Third Law Demonstration"
                                frameborder="0"
                                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
                                allowfullscreen
                                loading="lazy">
                            </iframe>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-center mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
                            <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NXk0EAC8jAQ" target="_blank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">
                                Watch on YouTube if video doesn't load
                            </a>
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="bg-white border-2 border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-4 mt-4">
                    <h4 class="font-bold mb-2">What to Look For:</h4>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-5">
                        <li>Action and reaction force pairs in various demonstrations</li>
                        <li>How forces are equal in magnitude but opposite in direction</li>
                        <li>Examples of Newton's Third Law in everyday situations</li>
                        <li>The relationship between mass and acceleration in action-reaction pairs</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Data Collection Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">DATA COLLECTION:</h3>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="min-w-full bg-white border rounded-lg">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th class="border p-2">Observation</th>
                            <th class="border p-2">Description</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="border p-2">Initial setup</td>
                            <td class="border p-2">
                                <textarea class="w-full p-1 border rounded" rows="2"></textarea>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="border p-2">During reaction</td>
                            <td class="border p-2">
                                <textarea class="w-full p-1 border rounded" rows="2"></textarea>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="border p-2">Bottle movement</td>
                            <td class="border p-2">
                                <textarea class="w-full p-1 border rounded" rows="2"></textarea>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="border p-2">Stopper movement</td>
                            <td class="border p-2">
                                <textarea class="w-full p-1 border rounded" rows="2"></textarea>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Conclusions Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">CONCLUSION:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">What happened in the experiment? Write a paragraph explaining what happened in terms of Newton's third law of motion. Make connections to the text.</p>
            <div class="mb-4">
                <textarea id="conclusion" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="6" placeholder="Write your conclusion here..."></textarea>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Analysis Questions Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">ANALYSIS QUESTIONS:</h3>
            <div class="space-y-4">
                <div>
                    <p class="font-bold">1. Identify the action and reaction forces in this experiment. Which is which?</p>
                    <textarea id="question1" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="2" placeholder="Your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p class="font-bold">2. Why did the bottle move in the opposite direction from the stopper?</p>
                    <textarea id="question2" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="2" placeholder="Your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p class="font-bold">3. How does this experiment demonstrate that the action and reaction forces are equal in magnitude?</p>
                    <textarea id="question3" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="2" placeholder="Your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p class="font-bold">4. Give three examples from everyday life where Newton's Third Law can be observed.</p>
                    <textarea id="question4" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="2" placeholder="Your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p class="font-bold">5. How is this experiment similar to how a rocket works?</p>
                    <textarea id="question5" class="w-full p-2 border rounded-lg" rows="2" placeholder="Your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Extension Activities Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700">EXTENSION ACTIVITIES:</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">Try these additional activities to further explore Newton's Third Law:</p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-8 mb-4">
                <li>Try the experiment with different amounts of vinegar and baking soda. How does this affect the reaction?</li>
                <li>Design a water rocket using a plastic bottle and compressed air. How does this demonstrate Newton's Third Law?</li>
                <li>Research how Newton's Third Law applies to rocket propulsion in space.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- Submit Section -->
        <section class="mb-8">
            <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-700 mb-4">SUBMIT YOUR RESULTS:</h3>
                <p class="mb-4">Click the button below to submit your experiment results to your teacher.</p>
                <button id="submitBtn" class="bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-4 rounded">
                    Submit Results
                </button>
                <p id="submissionMessage" class="mt-2 hidden"></p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
    // Load EmailJS
    (function() {
        emailjs.init("ct_s19oZHppLy9BlW");
    })();

    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
        // Store student information in local storage
        const studentNameInput = document.getElementById('studentName');
        const studentEmailInput = document.getElementById('studentEmail');
        const teacherEmailInput = document.getElementById('teacherEmail');

        // Load stored values if they exist
        if (localStorage.getItem('studentName')) {
            studentNameInput.value = localStorage.getItem('studentName');
        }
        if (localStorage.getItem('studentEmail')) {
            studentEmailInput.value = localStorage.getItem('studentEmail');
        }
        if (localStorage.getItem('teacherEmail')) {
            teacherEmailInput.value = localStorage.getItem('teacherEmail');
        }

        // Save values when they change
        studentNameInput.addEventListener('change', function() {
            localStorage.setItem('studentName', this.value);
        });
        studentEmailInput.addEventListener('change', function() {
            localStorage.setItem('studentEmail', this.value);
        });
        teacherEmailInput.addEventListener('change', function() {
            localStorage.setItem('teacherEmail', this.value);
        });

        // Handle form submission
        const submitBtn = document.getElementById('submitBtn');
        const submissionMessage = document.getElementById('submissionMessage');

        submitBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
            // Validate required fields
            const studentName = studentNameInput.value.trim();
            const studentEmail = studentEmailInput.value.trim();
            const teacherEmail = teacherEmailInput.value.trim();
            const conclusion = document.getElementById('conclusion').value.trim();

            if (!studentName || !studentEmail || !teacherEmail) {
                showMessage('Please fill in all required student and teacher information.', 'error');
                return;
            }

            if (!conclusion) {
                showMessage('Please complete your conclusion before submitting.', 'error');
                return;
            }

            // Collect data from the experiment
            const hypothesis = document.getElementById('hypothesis').value.trim();
            const observations = document.getElementById('observations').value.trim();
            
            const question1 = document.getElementById('question1').value.trim();
            const question2 = document.getElementById('question2').value.trim();
            const question3 = document.getElementById('question3').value.trim();
            const question4 = document.getElementById('question4').value.trim();
            const question5 = document.getElementById('question5').value.trim();

            // Get data table values
            const dataRows = document.querySelectorAll('table tbody tr');
            let dataValues = {};
            dataRows.forEach((row, index) => {
                const textarea = row.querySelector('textarea');
                if (textarea) {
                    dataValues[`observation_${index + 1}`] = textarea.value.trim() || 'Not provided';
                }
            });

            // Prepare comprehensive experiment data for email
            const experimentData = `
EXPERIMENT: Newton's Third Law

STUDENT: ${studentName}
EMAIL: ${studentEmail}

HYPOTHESIS:
${hypothesis || 'Not provided'}

GENERAL OBSERVATIONS:
${observations || 'Not provided'}

DETAILED OBSERVATIONS:
Initial Setup: ${dataValues.observation_1 || 'Not provided'}
During Reaction: ${dataValues.observation_2 || 'Not provided'}
Bottle Movement: ${dataValues.observation_3 || 'Not provided'}
Stopper Movement: ${dataValues.observation_4 || 'Not provided'}

ANALYSIS QUESTIONS:
1. ${question1 || 'Not answered'}
2. ${question2 || 'Not answered'}
3. ${question3 || 'Not answered'}
4. ${question4 || 'Not answered'}
5. ${question5 || 'Not answered'}

CONCLUSION:
${conclusion}
            `.trim();

            // Use proven EmailJS parameter structure from experiment3_2
            const templateParams = {
                student_name: studentName,
                student_email: studentEmail,
                experiment_title: 'Experiment 7.3: Newton\'s Third Law',
                hypothesis: hypothesis || 'Not provided',
                missing_elements: `Setup: ${dataValues.observation_1 || 'N/A'} | Reaction: ${dataValues.observation_2 || 'N/A'} | Bottle: ${dataValues.observation_3 || 'N/A'} | Stopper: ${dataValues.observation_4 || 'N/A'}`,
                conclusion: experimentData,
                subject: `Physical Science Experiment 7.3 Results - ${studentName}`
            };

            // Disable button and show loading state
            submitBtn.disabled = true;
            submitBtn.textContent = 'Submitting...';

            // Send email using EmailJS
            emailjs.send('service_ot1jg6s', 'template_y556rhr', templateParams)
                .then(function(response) {
                    console.log('SUCCESS!', response.status, response.text);
                    showMessage('Your experiment results have been submitted successfully!', 'success');
                    submitBtn.textContent = 'Submitted';
                })
                .catch(function(error) {
                    console.log('FAILED...', error);
                    showMessage('Failed to submit results. Please try again later.', 'error');
                    submitBtn.disabled = false;
                    submitBtn.textContent = 'Submit Results';
                });
        });

        function showMessage(message, type) {
            submissionMessage.textContent = message;
            submissionMessage.classList.remove('hidden', 'text-green-600', 'text-red-600');
            submissionMessage.classList.add(type === 'success' ? 'text-green-600' : 'text-red-600');
        }
    });
</script>
